7262-4a40-5650-84e4-b70a5816bde7" :question/math-academy-id "q-182642" :question/problem "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all {{field-9}}." :question/worked-solution "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every natural number $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every natural number $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)=2(3)+3=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+6)(3-2)=9(1)=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=3,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)=(k+6)(k-2).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=(k+7)(k-1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & =(k+6)(k-2)+(2k+5) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+4k-12+2k+5 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+6k-7 \\\\  & =(k+7)(k-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)$ is true for all $n\\ge 3.$"} :step/id #uuid "459c5a5c-82ad-5383-9f14-b32d642843f8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418392}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a169e12-7e4c-5e25-8147-73aad745a028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-S(k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418331} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f16e45-756f-5226-9e97-b731586b808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S(k)+2-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418353} {:answer/id #uuid "e236beed-88b1-54af-907e-d7f0041cdfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S(k)+1-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418361} {:answer/id #uuid "e315aeb0-337d-5bff-99f7-dec0f030ecf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-S(k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418362} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ab77ee-20d9-58f6-9099-417dec1cb15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S(k)+3-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418361} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d7e221-6d8a-5eb3-939e-8ee5b0d490ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fdbaf03b-caeb-5592-aca9-e0d9ca517487" :question/math-academy-id "q-182715" :question/problem "If $S(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}(2-i),$ then $S(k+1)=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to express the summation $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k).$\n\nLet ${a}_{i}$ for $i\\ge 0$ be a sequence, and let $S(k)$ denote the sum of the first $k+1$ terms.\n\n$$\nS(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i}\n$$\n\nWe can always express $S(k+1)$ in terms of $S(k)$ by writing the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${a}_{i}=2-i.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1}=2-(k+1)=1-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{k+1}(2-i)=({\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}(2-i))+(1-k),\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as \n\n$$\nS(k+1)=S(k)+1-k.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ebf63460-3ab8-5dad-9770-7340329a4251"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418388 :que