ee23-5aa9-9248-ce2fa8331e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5(1)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418366} {:answer/id #uuid "f72f7ba8-1839-50e8-96e0-e9d4e93fafd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1(5-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418369} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5313b3-1e44-53a0-a481-a7ddd62437df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418380} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a6e75d-f947-51ea-82be-89ed67ffed13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5k+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418320} {:answer/id #uuid "9f893393-7b97-5c37-bcb3-e7d6101e7e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(k+1)(5k+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418346} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c0ec16-680f-5323-906a-3d7f7c80cbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5k-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418350} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6e93d5-d92c-5a29-8c95-d4f18870278f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k(5k-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418346} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa081afb-2dea-5ed6-9075-a6b452c575a6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb1ce6e5-5388-5457-9a90-11e65945cce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-182681" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)=1(5-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)=k(5k-1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3) & =(k+1)(5(k+1)-1) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5k+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"} :step/id #uuid "f2f4d29e-fc61-5451-bca5-1fd86de9ebbb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418391}}] :activity/title "Mathematical Induction" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418319}]}
